// Human labels, one JSON object per line. Append-only.
//
// 45 labels across 21 scenarios, from one AI rater (see the provenance block
// below — what that set is and is not evidence of is stated there and in
// docs/CALIBRATION.md). Judge scores still gate nothing: kappa is computed on
// the first keyed run, and the gate additionally waits for the owner's own
// labels under their own handle.
//
// Format:
//   {"scenario":"<id>","rubric":"<criterion>","score":<int>,"labeller":"<handle>","date":"YYYY-MM-DD"}
//
// `labeller` is a handle, never a name or an email: this repository is public.
// `score` is on the criterion's own scale, from evals/rubrics/judge.yaml.
//
// Label WITHOUT reading the judge's scores first. Reading them produces agreement
// with the judge rather than a measurement of it, and the bias runs upward.
//
// ── First label set, 2026-08-15 ────────────────────────────────────────────
// Labeller: "claude-fable-5" — an AI assistant (Anthropic's Claude), NOT the
// judge and not the judge's model family. Disclosed here because the protocol
// says handles, and honesty says what kind of rater this was. These labels
// were produced from the transcripts in TestResults/narratives/ (the exact
// bytes the judge reads, dumped by NarrativeDumpTests) against the anchors in
// evals/rubrics/judge.yaml, BEFORE the judge had ever produced a score
// anywhere — no judge run had happened in this repository when they were
// written, so the anchoring-bias rule above was satisfiable by construction.
// docs/CALIBRATION.md records what this set is and is not evidence of: it
// demonstrates the protocol end to end and gives the first keyed run a
// measurement to compute, and it is not a human label set — the gate's spirit
// wants the owner's own labels appended under their own handle before judge
// scores gate anything.
